people haven't just died during the  pandemic because of the virus they died  of other causes too and that's made  clear by the most complete analysis so  far of excess deaths according to the  office for national statistics almost  134 000 more people died up to the end  of last year in england and wales than  expected from the five year average much  of that rise has been in men up more  than 17 in women the rise was just under  11  now if you look at the timeline you can  see how excess deaths have fluctuated  above and below the expected level which  we've zeroed on the chart  you can see big peaks in april 2020 in  the first wave and in january 2021  during the second but strip out deaths  due to coronavirus and the results get  more intriguing so there was still a  peak in non-covered excess deaths in  that first wave here in light blue  probably because of a lack of testing  meant some deaths really caused by the  virus were attributed to other medical  conditions on the death certificates but  excess death from other causes dipped  sharply during the second wave probably  partly because lockdown meant flu and  other respiratory diseases were at low  levels they then rose again and remained  high for the whole of the second half of  last year probably because those  respiratory diseases got going again in  the autumn and lots of people were  vulnerable  overall looking at the whole period  there were almost seven and a half  thousand fewer non-coveted death than  you would expect  what really stands out is the difficulty  people with underlying health conditions  had in accessing health care for example  deaths from dementia and alzheimer's  rose 71 at home but they fell 31  in hospitals and deaths from colon  cancer rose almost 37 at home but they  fell almost 19 in hospices when you look  at a map there's lots of variation in  excess deaths but no obvious geographic  pattern and no clear link with  deprivation but there were slightly more  than expected in rural areas and  slightly fewer in towns and cities so  how does all this compare with other  countries well earlier this month a  study in the lancet medical journal  looked at excess deaths around the world  and they found the uk ranked ninth in  western europe with a rate of just under  127 per 100 000 people rates were higher  in italy and spain but slightly lower in  germany and france the pandemic has  taken a dreadful toll on most countries  and understanding the statistics will be  key to learning the lessons
 

